  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe long-term pattern begins with the upper-level ridge \ntemporarily shifting westward allowing for greater influences of \nan upper-level through anchored along the eastern seaboard. On \nSunday, a few impulses of instability will move along the trough \nand eject into the CWA preceding a weak backdoor 'cold' front. \nThis will contribute to the possibility of more organized showers \nand thunderstorms Sunday afternoon through the overnight period. \nAs such, some of these storms could be strong to isolated severe \nwith Machine learning (ML) models highlighting some risk over the \nCWA. The main consideration is how much instability remains over \nour area during the period of increased forcing and that is to be \ndetermined. All that being said, the primary threat from these \nstorms would be strong to damaging wind gusts, frequent lightning,\nand heavy rainfall. \n\nBeyond the weekend, temperatures will start off a little cooler \non Monday with increased cloud cover behind our front as the \nupper- level ridge begins to pendulum back toward our area into \nthe first of next week. Expect temperatures to climb back into the\nlow to mid 90s through the week along with the isolated to \nscattered diurnal thunderstorm chances.",
